The “Great Wall of California” was not an official or grandiose wall. It was a nickname for an early U.S.–Mexico border fence built in the 1920s near San Diego–Tijuana.

It was built mainly to:
- curb Prohibition-era liquor smuggling from Mexico into California;
- reduce unauthorized border crossings;
- funnel traffic through official ports of entry after the U.S. created the Border Patrol in 1924.

So it was an early federal border-control fence, not a state-built “Great Wall,” and it was modest in scale compared with later border barriers.

